        <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6 font-sans border-b pb-4">
          How to Apply a Concrete Stain and Sealer Combo: Step-by-Step
        </h2>

        <p>
          A 2-in-1 product still demands careful surface preparation and technique — arguably more than a clear sealer, because any application error shows up as visible color unevenness rather than just a subtle sheen difference. Here is the process we followed across every product we tested.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 1: Strip Any Existing Sealer and Deep Clean</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Any existing sealer, curing compound, oil, or efflorescence must be completely removed before applying a stain-sealer combo — residual coatings block pigment penetration and cause blotchy, uneven color that no amount of extra coats will fix. Use a xylene-based stripper for old acrylic sealer, then degrease with an alkaline cleaner and pressure wash at 2,500-3,000 PSI. Allow 48 hours of dry weather minimum before staining. On penetrating polymerizing products like LastiSeal, this prep step is the single biggest determinant of a successful result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 2: Test a Discreet Section First</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Because these products color and seal in a single step, mistakes are far harder to correct than with a clear sealer. Always apply the product to a small, inconspicuous test area — a corner of the patio, a section behind a planter — and let it fully cure before committing to the entire surface. This is especially important with custom tint bases and with any porous or previously treated concrete, where absorption and final color can vary significantly from the product label's example photo.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="bg-gray-50 border-l-4 border-[#1E90FF] p-8 my-8 rounded-r-2xl">
          <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Step 3: Apply in Thin, Even, Overlapping Coats</h3>
          <p class="text-gray-600 m-0">
            Use a pump sprayer for the most even color distribution, back-rolling immediately with a foam or medium-nap roller to work the product into the surface and eliminate lap marks. Maintain a wet edge at all times — stopping mid-section and resuming after the product has begun to set is the most common cause of visible seams in a finished stain-sealer job. Most products recommend one to two coats; a second thin coat almost always produces a more even, richer result than trying to achieve full color in a single heavy coat.
          </p>
        </div>

